GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\queueunrated\codex_rounds_20260907.err:4897:GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28050:   "note": "*** THE #443 BADVAL CLASS AT SCALE -- THE SAME SHAPE AS `gdromcondlies`, WHICH WAS RATED HIGH AND HAS SINCE BEEN FIXED, AND AS `sgideprimop`, FILED HIGH TONIGHT. ***  **Four registers accept exactly ONE 64-bit value and end the host process on every other**, and two more accept only a single-bit mask.  The author documented the decision rather than hiding it: each `fatal()` reads *\"from the only implemented value\"*.  That is an honest TODO expressed as a host kill.  **A guest here is firmware** -- SRM or NetBSD bringing up the chipset -- so the reaching value is whatever that firmware writes, not an attacker's choice.  A model that implements one value and dies on the rest converts every unmodelled firmware revision into a host death.  *** SCOPE, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-CLAIMED: the two RANGE guards (`dev >= 21`, `ofs > len`) are a DIFFERENT question from the four VALUE guards and are not analysed here. ***  A bounds guard that kills may be protecting a real array, in which case the disposition is decline-not-delete, and deciding that needs the array geometry -- which is exactly the check that withdrew `sgideoob` as a false OOB.  Named, not judged.  **SINGLE CONSUMER: `machine_alpha.c:61`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.**  There is no alpha rig in this tree, which is why the file has escaped attention -- the same reason `dev_pcc2.c` did.  *No rig means no coverage rather than no bug.*  **COMPANION TO `pcc2widthkills`, AND DELIBERATELY EXAMINED SEPARATELY.**  Both files surfaced from one question -- is `dev_sgi_re.c` uniquely bad or uniquely looked at? -- but they are different classes: pcc2 is WIDTH kills (12 sites, all `len != 1`), this is VALUE kills.  Filing them as one row would have merged two fix designs, and the reopening rule already says a round takes one site unless the SAME fix design covers both.  *A census that finds two files at once is exactly where over-claiming starts.*  *** THIS ROW HAS NO RUNG-3 ATTEMPT RECORDED AT ALL, AND THERE IS A PLAUSIBLE TARGET IT NEVER NAMES.  Found 2026-08-31 by grepping the row for the attempt the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling requires. ***  The row reasons from *\"No rig means no coverage rather than no bug\"*, which is true about COVERAGE and is not the same claim as *no machine instantiates this device*.  **`src/machines/machine_alpha.c` calls `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` under `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:`, and that subtype is registered with the invocable alias `\"alphabook1\"`** (anchors: `grep -n 'device_add(machine, \"lca\")' src/machines/machine_alpha.c` and `grep -n 'alphabook1' src/machines/machine_alpha.c`).  So the prerequisite a rung-3 probe needs -- a real, committed, UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description that maps the device -- **exists**, and the invocation is `-E alpha -e alphabook1`.  *What is NOT established, and must not be assumed: whether that machine constructs far enough to accept a cold-debugger probe, and whether the four registers are reachable through its decode.  Those are exactly what the ATTEMPT is for.*  **The ruling's point is that the ask costs one invocation and nobody had made it** -- `witness: none` must mean *tried and could not*, never *did not think to*, and those two states must not render the same.  A seat that reports it cannot build one has given a real answer and the row proceeds as documented-only, with the attempt recorded.  *Filed as the next action on this row rather than done here: firing a probe contends with a measuring seat that was running when this was found, and a probe measured under load is the load-sensitive-oracle failure this project has already paid for once.*  *** THE STATED BLOCKER -- \"there is no rig\" -- IS NOT A REASON A RUNG-3 WITNESS IS IMPOSSIBLE, AND THIS PROJECT'S OWN MOST RECENT WITNESS REFUTES IT.  Measured 2026-08-31. ***  `#498`'s rung-3 witness for `sgidewidth` execs `gxemul -V -x -E sgi -e ip32 -M 64 0x80001000:<DUMMY>` and **`DUMMY` falls back to `/dev/null`** when `regress/dummy_kernel.bin` is absent (anchor: `grep -n 'DUMMY = ' regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py`).  **No disk image, no kernel, no boot -- a cold-debugger probe needs a machine that CONSTRUCTS and an empty load file, and that is all.**  Verified by running all four candidates under WSL with `<addr>:/dev/null`: `-E sgi -e ip32`, `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `-E mvme88k -e mvme187` and `-e mvme188` **all reach `machine:` and print a cpu line**.  *A rig is what a rung-4 BOOT needs.  Conflating the two is what left both of these rows ranked as witness-blocked when neither had been tried.*  **WHAT IS STILL NOT ESTABLISHED, and the attempt is exactly for this:** whether the debugger reaches these registers through the machine's real decode, and whether the observable differs pre- and post-fix.  Constructing is necessary, not sufficient.  *My first run of this test reported all three machines failing -- because I ran the WSL-built ELF from git-bash and got `cannot execute binary file`, while my own grep for `GXemul|machine:` matched the word `gxemul` INSIDE that error and scored it a healthy banner.  A broken apparatus plus a check that matched the error text nearly filed a confident \"these machines do not construct\".*  For this row the target is **`-E alpha -e alphabook1`**, which is the only subtype whose arm calls `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.  *** PASS-1 PACKET-FED PANEL, 2026-08-31 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346`).  THIS ROW'S OWN TITLE WAS WRONG, MY BRIEF CONTRADICTED ITSELF, AND BOTH ARE NOW SETTLED FROM THE SOURCE. ***  **(1) THE CENSUS.  The title said each register *accepts exactly ONE value*; that is true of ONE arm out of four.**  Derived, with the last constant looked up after glm named it as the settling fact:  `W_MASK0` accepts exactly `{0x700000}` -- one.  `W_BASE0` accepts `{0, 0x300800000}` -- **two**.  `IOC_CONF` kills iff `idata & ~1`, so it accepts `{0, 1}` -- **two**, and the predicate is *bit 0 only*, not a constant.  `TB_ENA` kills iff `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN`, and `alpha_lcareg.h` defines `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN 0x0000000000000080ULL` -- a SINGLE bit, so it accepts `{0, 0x80}` -- **two**.  Title corrected.  *This matters beyond tidiness: the de-escalation rule owes an accept row per predicate, and a detector built from `exactly one value` would pin the wrong accept side on three of the four arms.*  **glm's sharpest point is about WHICH value to pin:** for `W_BASE0` the accepted `0` is the *maximally ordinary* write -- zeroing a window -- so an accept row pinned to the exotic magic constant *misfires on the most legal write rather than an unusual one*.  **(2) MY BRIEF CONTRADICTED ITSELF AND THE SOURCE SETTLES IT AGAINST ME.**  Q2 asserted these fields *feed address-window decoding*; Q3's note said the emulator *does not otherwise model them*.  glm caught that both cannot be true and asked which.  Measured (`grep -n 'window_base_0|window_mask_0|tlb_enable|ioc_conf' src/devices/dev_lca.c`): `window_base_0`, `window_mask_0` and `tlb_enable` are **written and read back and nothing else** -- each appears exactly twice, as `odata = d->X` and `d->X = idata`.  **Only `ioc_conf` is CONSUMED**, at `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`.  *** SO THE FIX DESIGN SPLITS, AND IT WOULD NOT HAVE WITHOUT THIS CHECK. ***  Three of the four arms are pure BACKED ACCEPT storage -- a decline that keeps the store harms nothing, because no emulator code reads the field.  **The fourth is different**: `ioc_conf`'s low bit selects the bus, and its guard is `idata & ~1`, so a rejected value's bit 0 still reaches the consumer.  glm's *\"a decline that keeps the store is an accept with a complaint, so validate BEFORE storing and leave the previous value\"* is right **for that arm specifically**, and is over-general for the other three.  **(3) `idata` IS `uint64_t`** (`memory_readmax64`), so `0x300800000` is representable and glm's width worry is answered -- the comparison is reachable, not dead code.  **(4) Reachability stays a READING, correctly.**  All three seats declined to say whether these are the only values REAL HARDWARE accepts or merely the only ones this emulator was developed against, and read the code's own wording (`TODO`, *\"differs from the expected values\"*) as supporting the second.  There is no LCA datasheet in `_scratchpad/`.  *That is the honest form and it is flagged as a reading rather than a fact.*\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-08-31 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***  evidence: dev_lca.c :203 :222 :237 :252 :276 :291 (six guards); :129 inside a comment block\n\n*** THREE WRONG RECORDS ON THIS ROW, found by the rung-3 seat and RE-VERIFIED BY READING dev_lca.c before being written here. ***\n\n(1) SCOPE: there are SIX value-guarded arms, not four.  Verified at dev_lca.c :203 (idata & ~1, IOC_CONF), :222 (idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN, TB_ENA), :237 (W_BASE0), :252 (W_MASK0), :276 (W_BASE1), :291 (W_MASK1).  OUTSTANDING_BUGS scoped four-plus-two-masks (= the correct six); the LEDGER's pass-1 census picked a DIFFERENT four and silently dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1.  A detector built from the ledger's four ships NO ACCEPT ROW for those two, which the de-escalation clause requires per predicate.\n\n(2) THE EARLIER TITLE CORRECTION IS ITSELF WRONG.  It says the one-value claim is `true of ONE arm out of four'.  Against all six, THREE arms accept exactly one value: W_MASK0 {0x700000}, W_BASE1 {0x240000000}, W_MASK1 {0x3ff00000}.  W_BASE0 accepts two, IOC_CONF {0,1}, TB_ENA {0,0x80}.  **The original title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it** -- this project's own rule that a correction carries more authority than the error and therefore earns a SECOND reading.\n\n(3) hold_question says TWELVE live exit(1).  It is ELEVEN: `grep -c` returns 12, but dev_lca.c:129 is inside a /* ... */ block.  The row's own `witness' field already said eleven, so the row contradicted itself.\n\nCONSUMPTION, confirmed and stronger than filed: five of the six guarded arms are PURE STORAGE (each field appears exactly twice -- odata = d->X and d->X = idata).  `ioc_conf' is the sole consumer, at dev_lca.c:121 `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1'.",
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\queueunrated\codex_rounds_20260907.err:4901:GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28138:   "state_note": "*** RECORDS CORRECTION 2026-09-06, from RE-DERIVING this row's own citations instead of trusting them.  THE\nHEADLINE COUNT (eleven live terminating calls) IS RIGHT.  THE ENUMERATION UNDER IT IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, AND THE\nFIRST ONE CHANGES THE FIX DESIGN. ***\n\n**1.  `dev >= 21` IS NOT A LIVE KILL.  IT IS ALREADY DE-FATALISED -- AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN FIX PRECEDENT.**\n\nThe row lists it among \"two range guards that also kill\".  The source says otherwise: the guard is at `:127`, its\nbody is a COMMENTED-OUT `fatal()` + `exit(1)` at `:128-129`, and what actually executes is `dev = 0;` at `:130`.\n\n```c\n\tif (dev >= 21) {\n\t\t/*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);  */\n\t\tdev = 0;\n\t}\n```\n\nSomeone has already made, in this very file, the decision this row exists to make: **delete the kill, substitute a\nsafe default, keep going.**  That is a TRANSFERRED WARRANT from inside the same file, which the six-member\noption-set rule says to go looking for -- *\"grep the fork for an existing idiom; the tree's own precedents are part\nof the design space\"* -- and which nobody had found because the row was read rather than re-derived.  So only ONE\nrange guard is live: `ofs > len` at `:163`.\n\n**2.  THE ENUMERATION COVERED 8 OF THE 11, AND THE THREE IT MISSED ARE NOT VALUE GUARDS AT ALL.**\n\nRe-derived by `grep -n 'exit(1)' dev_lca.c` minus the commented one (12 matches, 11 live), the eleven are:\n\n| line | class | condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 73  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_assert` -- bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)`, **NO GUARD** |\n| 85  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_deassert` -- likewise |\n| 134 | BUS | `bus != 0`, i.e. `d->ioc_conf & 1` |\n| 163 | RANGE | `ofs > len` |\n| 206 | VALUE | `idata & ~1` (IOC_CONF) |\n| 226 | VALUE | `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` |\n| 241 | VALUE | `idata != 0 && idata != 0x300800000` |\n| 256 | VALUE | `idata != 0x700000` |\n| 280 | VALUE | `idata != 0x240000000` |\n| 295 | VALUE | `idata != 0x3ff00000` |\n| 315 | CATCH-ALL | `default:` -- any unimplemented ioc offset, read OR write |\n\n**THE TWO INTERRUPT STUBS ARE PLAUSIBLY THE MOST SEVERE OF THE ELEVEN, AND A ROW FRAMED AS A VALUE-GUARD CLASS\nCOULD NOT SEE THEM.**  They are not a wrong value being written -- they are an ENTIRE UNIMPLEMENTED PATH with no\npredicate at all, so *any* device asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it\nwrites.  The four \"accepts exactly one value\" registers need a firmware revision that writes something else; these\ntwo need only that an interrupt is asserted.\n\n*A row named after one class hides the members of the others.  That is this project's cell-not-class pattern\nwearing an enumeration instead of a detector -- the same shape the m88kexcdeliver chain hit four times.*\n\n**WHAT IS UNCHANGED:** the severity (HIGH), the single consumer (`machine_alpha.c`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`),\nthe absence of any alpha rig, and the observation that a guest here is FIRMWARE so the reaching value is whatever\nSRM or NetBSD writes.  The scope caveat about range-vs-value guards also stands and is now sharper, because there\nis only one live range guard to reason about.\n\n--- the original note follows, unaltered ---\n\n\n=== DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-06 (codex / GPT-6 Astra at max, read-only).  Answer preserved at\n=== regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt.  IT CORRECTED THREE OF THE FIVE PREMISES I HAD\n=== JUST RE-DERIVED, and one of the corrections changes what this round may even choose from.\n\n**SEVERITY ORDER CONFIRMED: the two INTERRUPT STUBS first** -- but \"provisionally; firmware execution order is\nunproved\", which is the honest qualifier my own framing lacked.\n\n*** A SEVENTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: IMPLEMENT. ***  The carrier's set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+LATCHED\nANNOUNCE / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT -- all six presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION.  For the two\ninterrupt stubs none of them applies: *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete.\"*  A stub is not a\nrefusal to service a value; it is an unimplemented PATH, and the disposition is to write the code.  This is the\noption-set completeness rule paying off exactly as it is meant to -- the member was found by a seat reading the\ntree, not by argument.\n\n*** AND THE FAULT MEMBER IS NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE AT ALL. ***  `dev_unreadable.c` works because\n`memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access **for MIPS and M88K, with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT**.\nSo the idiom this fork installs ten times in `machine_sgi.c` does not transfer here, and any round reaching for\nFAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  *A precedent that is real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby\ntransferable; that is the warrant-transfer rule, and it took a seat opening `memory_rw.c` to see it.*\n\n**AND A FINDING ABOUT THE ALPHA PORT THAT OUTRANKS THIS ROW:** the Alpha interrupt callbacks merely set\n`irq_asserted`, and **the execution loop never consumes that flag**.  So implementing the two stubs would not by\nitself deliver an interrupt -- removing the exits cannot complete the path.  Filed as its own question rather\nthan folded in here.\n\n## THE PREMISE AUDIT, and I was wrong three times\n\n* **PREMISE 1 HOLDS.**  Twelve textual occurrences, eleven outside comments.\n* **PREMISE 2's REMOVAL IS REAL; ITS \"USABLE PRECEDENT\" CONCLUSION IS WRONG.**  I read the commented-out\n  `dev >= 21` kill plus its live `dev = 0` as the file's own answer, transferable to this round.  It is not: it\n  **ALIASES \"no selected device\" to DEVICE ZERO**, which is harmless today only because device 7 is the sole\n  installed device and slot 0 happens to be empty.  Populate slot 0 and it is a latent alias defect.  *So the\n  question I asked -- is this a sound precedent or a latent defect of its own -- has the second answer, and my\n  brief had already leaned on the first.*\n* **PREMISE 3's CLASS COUNTS HOLD, ITS SINGLETON CLAIM DOES NOT.**  `W_BASE0` accepts **TWO** values, `0` and\n  `0x300800000` (`idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`), so \"four registers accepting exactly ONE value\" is\n  wrong -- only **three** window guards accept a single value.  *This one is mine twice over: the original row\n  note said it, I re-derived the enumeration tonight and reproduced it, and the guard's own text was in my\n  terminal when I did.*\n* **PREMISE 4 HOLDS** as one machine-setup consumer (AlphaBook 1), with no dedicated boot rig -- **but\n  \"never exercised\" DOES NOT FOLLOW**: a generic constructor sweep exists (`gate_asan_sweep.sh`), without\n  proving coverage of these branches.  My \"no rig means no coverage\" was too strong.\n* **PREMISE 5 IS AN UNSUPPORTED RESTRICTION.**  I said a guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is\n  whatever SRM or NetBSD writes.  Guest KERNEL stores use the same MMIO path, and interrupt callbacks arise from\n  DEVICE events -- neither is firmware.\n\n## DISPOSITIONS IT WOULD TAKE, per class\n* **INTERRUPT STUB (2):** IMPLEMENT forwarding and CPU interrupt delivery.\n* **BUS (1):** implement Type-1 decoding then ACCEPT through `bus_pci`, using its existing absent-device\n  handling.  `ioc_conf & 1` selects the CONFIGURATION-CYCLE TYPE and is **not the decoded bus number** -- and\n  deleting this exit alone leaves `dev` uninitialised.\n* **RANGE (1):** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with initialised read results and no forwarded side effects.  Note\n  `ofs` is at most three, **so this guard cannot fire for four- or eight-byte accesses** -- changing `>`\n  mechanically is not a design.\n* **VALUE (6):** ACCEPT documented register fields using the existing backing, latched announce for the missing\n  DMA/TLB effects.  **Every guarded assignment already happens BEFORE its check**, so no invented backing is\n  needed -- but readback alone does not implement translation.\n* **CATCH-ALL (1):** split documented registers (`STAT0`/`STAT1`) from genuine holes; FAULT only for confirmed\n  holes and only once Alpha exception delivery exists.\n\n**WHAT IT COULD NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly:** source shows connected interrupt paths and conditional MMIO paths\nfor the other nine sites, but **no particular SRM/NetBSD execution of any terminating site**.  Firmware\nreachability and failure frequency remain unmeasured, so the severity order is provisional on exactly that.\n\n\n*** THE SEVERITY WARRANT THIS ROW WAS MISSING: THE REJECTED VALUES INCLUDE A DOCUMENTED ONE, AND THE\nSTORE LANDS BEFORE THE PROCESS ENDS.  Found by the codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt),\nverified against the source and the header before recording. ***\n\nEach of the three window guards has this shape:\n\n    d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS\n    if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends\n\n**And zero is a documented encoding.**  From src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:\n\n    #define IOC_W_MASK_1M   0x0000000000000000ULL   /* 1MB window */\n\nSo a guest selecting the SMALLEST DOCUMENTED WINDOW SIZE ends the emulator.  The same argument covers\nW_BASE1, whose one accepted constant 0x240000000 carries IOC_W_BASE_WEN (0x200000000, *Window enable*) --\n**so a driver DISABLING a window, by writing a base with WEN clear, is killed too.**\n\n*** THIS IS #504's WARRANT AT A SECOND DEVICE: documented values that the model kills on. ***  It is not a\njunk-value guard; it is a whitelist of ONE that excludes documented members of its own encoding.  codex adds\nthat all three registers start at zero through constructor initialisation, so the rejected value is also the\nRESET value.\n\n**AND THE RANGE SITE IS FAR MORE REACHABLE THAN THIS ROW SAID.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len'.  Reading\nDEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;' -- **ofs is guest ADDRESS BITS 5-6**,\nnot a written value.  So an ordinary 1-byte access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the\nprocess.  That is address decoding, not a value whitelist, and it belongs in a different disposition class\nfrom the six value guards.  The rest of that function is filed separately as [[lcaisalane]].\n\n*** DISPOSITION PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition_20260907.txt).  IT NARROWS MY REACHABILITY CLAIM,\nCONFIRMS TWO OTHERS, AND SUPPLIES THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR -- INCLUDING\nCONFLICTING ONES, WHICH IS WHAT I ASKED IT TO SURFACE. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Disposition 5 is unavailable through Alpha's failed-device-access path.  Five candidate rounds fit\na uniform VALUE-refusal policy.  The tree supplies CONFLICTING precedents, especially for lane handling and\nstoring unsupported values.\"*\n\n*** MY `AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE ENDS THE EMULATOR' CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND I HAVE REPEATED IT IN THREE\nPLACES. ***  codex: *\"they read zero INITIALLY, then echo accepted writes.  Writing back the initial zero\nkills the last three; an arbitrary RMW does not necessarily kill them -- its resulting value matters.\"*\n\n**The precise claim is the INITIAL-ZERO readback**: before any accepted write, `W_MASK0', `W_BASE1' and\n`W_MASK1' read 0 and accept only one non-zero constant each, so a read-modify-write performed on a\nfreshly-reset device writes 0 back and ends the process.  **Once a valid value has been written the register\nechoes it**, and an RMW of that value is accepted.  So the window is early -- driver probe or\ninitialisation -- rather than any RMW at any time.  That is still a routine thing for a driver to do at\nattach, and it is still reached with a documented encoding (`IOC_W_MASK_1M` = 0), but the sentence I wrote\nwas wider than the fact.\n\n**CONFIRMED, both of which I had asserted:** every VALUE arm STORES BEFORE CHECKING; and the accepted sets\nare exactly as filed (`IOC_CONF' {0,1}, `IOC_TB_ENA' {0,0x80}, `W_BASE0' {0,0x300800000}, and the three\nsingle-value ones).\n\n**DISPOSITION 5 IS UNAVAILABLE -- now established from a third direction.**  The carrier already recorded it\ngenerally, [[alphafaultclaim]] reached it from the instruction side, and this reaches it from the\nfailed-device-access path.  **Any proposal for a bus-error refusal on an Alpha device owes Alpha exception\ndelivery first**, which is a far larger round than the one it would be riding on.\n\n**THE PRECEDENT I ASKED FOR, AND IT IS AN EXACT ONE:** `cpu_sh.c' defines `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()' and\n`sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()' with EMPTY `/* TODO */' bodies -- *\"an exact unimplemented-callback precedent\nfor 2, ignore silently\"* -- and `core/interrupt.c' defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert' callbacks,\nthough those represent intentionally absent connections rather than unimplemented ones.  **So the two\nINTERRUPT STUBS have a direct in-tree answer** and do not need disposition 7 after all, which is what I had\nassumed they needed.\n\n**AND IT REPORTS THE PRECEDENTS CONFLICT** -- especially on lane handling and on whether to store an\nunsupported value.  That is the more useful answer: it means the choice is genuinely open at those sites\nrather than settled by the tree, so a round there must argue rather than transfer.\n\n**SCOPE: FIVE candidate rounds** under a uniform VALUE-refusal policy -- consistent with the earlier read's\n*\"this needs several rounds\"* and finer than it.\n\n**WHERE THE OVER-BROAD FORM SURVIVES, named because it cannot be edited:** commit `de96f11`'s MESSAGE\nsays *\"each reads back 0 while its guard accepts one non-zero constant, and AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE\nENDS THE EMULATOR on all three\"*.  A commit message is immutable, so the house rule -- correct the false\nrecord IN PLACE -- cannot be followed literally.  **The next best thing is to point at it from the record a\nreader will reach**, which is this row.  The narrowed claim above is the operative one: the kill needs the\nINITIAL zero readback, not any RMW at any time.\n\n*** THE PREREQUISITE IS DISCHARGED.  `#506` LANDED THIS AFTERNOON. ***\n\nThis row's HOLD-Q asked: *\"given that every row must match the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit\nstatus, and given that `lcaconfwidth` means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the\ntext-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix land FIRST, making it a\nprerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?\"*\n\n**It landed.**  `#506` repaired both narrowing sites in `dev_lca.c` -- `:205` (IOC_CONF) and the\n`default:` arm -- to `0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, matching the five siblings.  Measured by\n`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`: **HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6, same script**, with `kill width` now reading\n`0x100000000` instead of `0x0`.\n\n> **So the text oracle is now sound at the IOC_CONF arm, and the ordering question in the HOLD-Q is\n> answered by events rather than by adjudication.**  What remains of that HOLD-Q is its SECOND half,\n> untouched: *the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier\n> census dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1` -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.*\n\n*The interrupt-stub confound is also unchanged and still governs the witness design:\n`lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal()`+`exit(1)` with the ISA PIC\nchain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the same status as the value guards under\ntest.  That is why the rows must match text, which is why `#506` was the prerequisite.*"
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\queueunrated\codex_rounds_20260907.err:4904:GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28181:   "state_note": "\n\n*** CODEX REFUSAL #7, 2026-09-07, ON THE `lcaconfwidth` CENSUS BRIEF -- AND MY OWN LAUNCHER\nREPORTED IT AS A CLEAN RUN.  Two defects, both mine, both of the check-that-cannot-fail family. ***\n\n    codex rc=1        stdout 0 bytes        stderr 768,161 bytes\n    stderr tail:  ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.\n    tokens used:  108,432\n\n**The seat did 108k tokens of real work first** -- the stderr shows it reading `dev_osiop.c` and\nenumerating diagnostic sites -- and the filter discarded the answer at the end.  So this is not a\nseat that declined the task; it is a seat whose completed answer was destroyed on the way out.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 1: the content check read the WRONG STREAM.**  It grepped the `.txt` for refusal\ntext.  A content-filter refusal writes NOTHING to stdout and puts the ERROR line on STDERR, so the\ncheck printed `refusal hits: 0` **on a refusal**.  A check that looks in the wrong stream returns\nthe same answer whether or not the thing it tests is true -- which is the exact shape this project\nnamed after `panel.sh`'s phantom re-fire.\n\n**LAUNCHER DEFECT 2: the script exited 0 while holding `rc=1`.**  It captured codex's status into a\nvariable, printed it, and then ended on a successful command.  **The harness reported the task\nCOMPLETED.**  That is the failure-laundered-into-a-clean-completion class, and this project has\nthree prior instances (a `diff` under `set -e`, a Windows path to `wsl`, a truncated preserved log).\n\n> **BOTH FIXED in `cxlaunch.sh`, which greps BOTH STREAMS and exits 7 on a refusal, 8 on a wall, 6\n> on an empty answer with neither, and `rc` otherwise.**  A refused seat can no longer render as a\n> completed one.\n\n**THE TRIGGER, added to the recorded set.**  The brief asked, of a kill diagnostic, *\"say whether a\n64-bit value exists that trips the guard and prints as something the guard would accept -- give the\ndiscriminating value where one exists.\"*  That is a diagnostic-correctness question, but its SHAPE\nis *find the input that defeats the check*, and it sits beside an enumeration of `exit(1)` sites.\n**Chain-shaped content, whatever the question form** -- the trigger this project already recorded,\nmet again.\n\n**ROUTED TO GROK**, which the carrier records as the working path for a refused brief and which has\nanswered three of them, finding something material in each.\n\n*** GROK CENSUS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/grok_census_20260907.txt`), after\ncodex refused the brief.  IT CORRECTED MY COUNT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS AND NARROWED THE FIX TO TWO\nLINES. ***\n\n**MY CENSUS MISSED A SPELLING.**  I grepped `(int)idata`; the tree also writes **`(int) idata`**\nwith a space.  Real total **309 sites in 70 files**, not 226 -- and 309 itself includes\nCOMMENTED-OUT casts (`dev_clmpcc.c:125`, `dev_dc7085.c:236`, `dev_dec21143.c:914/1072`), so the live\nfigure is lower again.\n\n**AND MY PROXIMITY SET WAS SHORT: 18 live sites within +/-3 of an `exit(1)`, not 15.**  The three I\nmissed are `g2` CHAN_ENABLE, `g2` XFER_ENABLE and the `osiop` default -- a blank line inside the\nwindow is still inside the window.  grok also priced the string-vs-call distinction: a scanner that\ncounted comment text would report **30** (18 live + 2 commented-out `exit(1)` + 10 `dev_pvr.c`\n*\"don't exit(1)\"* comments).\n\n*** THE FINDING THAT SETS THE DISPOSITION, and it is the opposite of a tree-wide class: ***\n\n    (uint64_t)idata  IS next to a live exit(1) FIVE times, ALL IN dev_lca.c\n        225/226  TB_ENA      240/241  W_BASE0     255/256  W_MASK0\n        279/280  W_BASE1     294/295  W_MASK1\n    (uint32_t)idata  13 sites, NONE within +/-3 of a live exit(1)\n    (long)idata       1 site, not near one\n    (int32_t) / (unsigned int) idata   0\n\n**Those five LCA guards already print the FULL 64-bit value.**  So `dev_lca.c` is mostly correct\nalready, and the narrowing print survives at exactly **two** sites in it -- `:205` (IOC_CONF, the\nexemplar) and `:313`.  **The repair is two lines in one file, matching the idiom its own siblings\ntwelve lines away already use.**  That is a transferred warrant from inside the same function, which\nis the strongest kind this project recognises, and it makes the sibling-disposition check answer FOR\na local fix here -- the opposite of how it answered for [[sgirebufshiftub]].\n\n**Also found: the filed PVR 'sharpest instance' is ALREADY DE-FATALISED by `#166`** -- the fourth\nalready-fixed claim surfaced today.\n\n> **ORDERING vs [[lcabadval]]:** that row's witness must match `fatal()` TEXT rather than exit status\n> (because the interrupt stubs kill with the same status), and IOC_CONF is one of the arms it would\n> read.  With the repair confined to two lines whose siblings already do it right, taking this first\n> is cheap and removes the oracle's one unsound arm.\n\n*** REACHABILITY ESTABLISHED BY READING, 2026-09-07 -- so a rung-3 witness is a construction\nproblem rather than an open question. ***\n\n    alpha_lcareg.h      LCA_IOC_BASE 0x180000000 ; LCA_IOC_CONF = BASE + 0x020 = 0x180000020\n    dev_lca.c :359-361  memory_device_register(..., \"lca_ioc\", LCA_IOC_BASE, 0x20000000,\n                        dev_lca_ioc_access, ..., DM_DEFAULT, NULL)   <- NO length constraint\n    dev_lca.c :188      idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)     <- takes the full width\n    machine_alpha.c :61 device_add(machine, \"lca\") -- inside `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:` ONLY\n\n**And the technique is already proven on that exact machine.**  `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py`\nran today on an unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1` at **7/9**, with `live`, `exec` and `palgate`\ngreen; it builds K0SEG addresses as `0xfffffc0000000000 | x` and K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x000003ffffffffff`, so `k0(0x180000020)` reaches the register with no page tables.\n\n**The 64-bit question is answered too:** Alpha `STQ` is opcode `0x2d` with `loadstore_type = 3`\n(quadword) -- established in the grok harness review against `cpu_alpha_instr.c` -- so a guest `stq`\ndelivers `len = 8` and `idata` holds bit 32.  **The discriminating store is therefore\n`0x100000000` to `k0(0x180000020)`: the guard `idata & ~1` fires, and `(int)idata` prints `0x0` --\na value that same guard ACCEPTS.**\n\n> **THE CONTROL THIS WITNESS OWES, and it is the device form of the trap that nearly voided the\n> Alpha harness:** if `k0(0x180000020)` does NOT land on the LCA window, the store goes to unmapped\n> space and **nothing happens at all** -- no message, no exit -- which is indistinguishable from\n> *the guard did not fire*.  So the probe needs a positive control proving the store reached\n> `dev_lca`: a legal `0x1` write that SURVIVES, and/or a read-back of `d->ioc_conf` through\n> `LCA_IOC_CONF`.  **That question is out to the grok seat rather than settled here**, because\n> whether either is sufficient alone is exactly the judgement this project keeps getting wrong.\n\n*Design brief: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/WITNESS_brief.md`.  Sent to grok rather than codex --\ncodex refused the sibling census brief on its content filter, and the shape 'find the value that\ndefeats the check' is the recorded trigger even when the subject is a printf format specifier.*\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN, 2026-09-07 (`grok_witness2_20260907.txt`).  VERDICT: CONSTRUCTIBLE as a\nrung-3 witness on stock `alphabook1`.  It corrected FOUR of my premises, one of which was a wrong\nrecord in this row's own OUTSTANDING_BUGS entry. ***\n\n**1. THE RECOMMENDED FIX WAS WRONG, AND SO WAS ITS WARRANT.**  The entry said `0x%llx` with a\n`(long long)` cast, *\"the file's own idiom elsewhere\"*.  `dev_lca.c` uses **`PRIx64` five times and\n`%llx` never**, and the siblings **KEEP** the cast as `(uint64_t)idata`.  My design brief compounded\nit by saying to DROP the cast -- a third wrong form.  Corrected in place.\n\n**2. `load_const` CANNOT BUILD EITHER OPERAND.**  LDA/LDAH make 32-bit sign-extended constants; the\nK0SEG address needs bits 63..42 and the test value needs bit 32.  **A probe using `load_const` for\neither is not this witness.**  The working bootstrap is the one the Alpha harness already uses:\n`put d` the 64-bit words into RAM below 64 MB, then guest `ldq` them while `ptbr` is still 0.\n\n**3. THE MISS MODE IS NOT ONLY 'UNMAPPED -> NOTHING'.**  Offset 0 is `LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE), which\n`break`s with no kill -- so a store to `k0(0x180000000)` is **silent while INSIDE the LCA window**.\nUnmapped and HAE both read back 0, which is why the control has to distinguish them.\n\n**4. TWO ROUTES THAT LOOK LIKE CONTROLS AND ARE NOT:**\n  * **debugger `dump` cannot read the register.**  `dump` calls `memory_rw` with `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and\n    LCA is `DM_DEFAULT` rather than `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS`, so the handler is **SKIPPED** and\n    the caller gets the pre-zeroed buffer -- *the unmapped signature, produced by a hit.*\n  * **`put d` of the killing value is not rung-3.**  `store_64bit_word` goes through `memory_rw` with\n    `CACHE_DATA` and no `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, so it DOES enter the handler and `exit(1)` from the\n    debugger -- a real kill, by no guest instruction.\n\n> **THE CONTROL THAT WORKS: a guest `stq` of `0x1` followed by a guest `ldq`, same decode, BEFORE\n> the killing store.**  It is the analogue of the Alpha harness's `swpctx mapped` row.  Neither it\n> nor the kill text is sufficient alone -- the round-trip proves the window is THIS arm and not HAE\n> or unmapped; the text `IOC_CONF: 0x0` proves the arm fired and that a 32-bit zero store could not\n> have produced that pair.\n\n**Q3 CONFIRMED IN DETAIL, and it hardens S2:** `stq` is opcode `0x2d` -> `loadstore_type 3` -> `LS_Q`;\n`host_store` is NULL for a device page, so it falls to `LS_GENERIC_N` and calls `memory_rw(..., 8,\nMEM_WRITE)`; the memblock granule is 1 MB so `0x20+8` crosses nothing; Alpha is little-endian and\n`memory_readmax64` rebuilds the value.  **And the guard's own arithmetic is load-bearing:** `~1` is a\nsigned `int` (-2) converting to `0xfffffffffffffffe`, **so it sees bit 32 -- `~1U` would NOT, and\nthat is a different mutant worth building.**  The same function already compares against\n`0x300800000ULL`, so 64-bit values are this handler's native width.\n\n**One honest weakening:** `(int)0x100000000 == 0` is IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED, not guaranteed by C99.\nGCC/Clang truncate, so the witness holds on this build -- but the row should not claim a language\nguarantee.\n\n*** AND THE 309 FIGURE WAS ALREADY IN THIS ROW'S OWN ENTRY, DATED 2026-08-31. ***  I ran a census\ntoday, got 226, treated it as new, and was corrected to 309 by a seat -- while\n`OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` had recorded **\"309 casts across 70 device files\"** five days earlier, with\nits deriver.  **The unread-review class, in the file I was writing about.**  (Its companion figure,\n45 host-kill sites, is not in conflict with today's 18: the two used different window sizes, and\nneither states one, which is the reason they cannot be reconciled.)\n\n*** WITNESS BUILT AND RUN THE SAME HOUR THE DESIGN LANDED.  HEAD 4/6. ***\n\n    live          alive   alive          ok    the control session survives the legal write\n    ioc rt        0x1     0x1            ok    guest stq 0x1 then guest ldq, SAME decode\n    kill fired    yes     yes            ok    the IOC_CONF arm announced -- not HAE, not unmapped\n    kill width    0x0     0x100000000    FAIL  the message must name the value that ACTUALLY killed\n    kill notzero  0x0     NOT 0x0        FAIL  0x0 is a value this guard ACCEPTS\n    died          died    died           ok    exit(1) really ran\n\nAll four of grok's design corrections are implemented and each is a comment in the probe: the\n64-bit operands are planted with `put d` and loaded with `ldq` (`load_const` cannot build them);\nthe read-back is a guest `ldq` (debugger `dump` would skip a `DM_DEFAULT` handler and hand back the\npre-zeroed buffer -- the unmapped signature from a hit); the killing store is a guest `stq` (a\n`put d` would kill from the debugger and not be rung-3); and `ioc rt` distinguishes the real arm\nfrom HAE at offset 0, which `break`s silently INSIDE the window.\n\n*** AND MY FIRST DRAFT HAD THE ORACLE POLARITY BACKWARDS -- it scored 6/6 on the committed build. ***\nIt asserted the DEFECT as the passing state, so it read as success and would have gone RED the\nmoment the bug was fixed.  **The ladder requires a witness to show the symptom pre-fix and go green\npost-fix WITH THE SAME SCRIPT**, which is clause (i).  The sibling `alpha_harness_probe.py` had it\nright (7/9) and this one did not, in the same tree, on the same day.\n\n> *A witness that passes on the committed build is a detector-of-the-defect wearing a witness's\n> clothes.  The tell is cheap and I nearly missed it: **ask what the row does after the fix.**  If\n> the answer is 'goes red', the polarity is inverted whatever the score says.*\n\n**The repair is now fully specified and unblocks [[lcabadval]]:** `0x%\" PRIx64` with\n`(uint64_t)idata`, copying the five sibling arms verbatim; no gate, hygiene grep or probe in\n`regress/` matches this message's text, so changing it turns nothing else red.\n\n*** `#506` APPLIED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-07.  THE ROUND IS COMPLETE AND HELD FOR THE 9th. ***\n\n    HEAD  4/6   probe_head_20260907.out   `kill width' 0x0,          `kill notzero' 0x0\n    fix   6/6   probe_fix_20260907.out    `kill width' 0x100000000,  `kill notzero' wider\n\n**Same script, red before and green after** -- ladder clause (i) discharged by the pair rather than\nasserted.  Both sites repaired: `:205` (IOC_CONF, the sharp instance -- guard on the VALUE) and\n`:319` (the `default:` arm, weaker -- guard on the OFFSET, so its narrowed value is wrong without\nbeing one the guard accepts).\n\nPropagated to all four trees; `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt with the\ntwo-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH on each, `est/` part 2 N/A.  **Nothing committed** -- the Fable hold\nstands, and this round now waits as FINISHED WORK rather than as a proposal."
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\queueunrated\codex_rounds_20260907.err:6027:@@ -201,8 +201,14 @@ DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\queueunrated\codex_rounds_20260907.err:6043:@@ -310,7 +316,7 @@ DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_minimax.txt:1:**VERDICT**: **A for all six.** Premise 6 is conditionally sound — it holds IF premise 2 holds (no fourth read of the five window/TB_ENA fields). The brief treats all six as one block, but **CONF is structurally different** (it has a consumer: `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`), and the argument for the five doesn't transfer cleanly. For the five, A is right because there is no consumer to protect and A preserves register-file fidelity (write X, read X, which hardware does). For CONF, A is *even more clearly* right: the consumer uses only bit 0, so storing a rejected value leaves the consumer correct; **B can silently break the consumer** if the old `ioc_conf` had a different bit 0. B is the wrong fix everywhere. The single fact that decides the fork is premise 2 — whether any tree code reads the five fields outside their readback sites.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_minimax.txt:10:| 2 | Five fields pure storage; `ioc_conf` has one consumer | **REASONED** | The counts (3 each for the five; 4 for `ioc_conf`, with the fourth being `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`) are stated by the author as re-verified with a full denominator. The negative claim "no DMA-translation consumer" follows from the count but is not independently checkable from this packet. |
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_minimax.txt:11:| 3 | IOC readback returns the stored value | **READ** | `odata = d->ioc_conf;` is in the packet. Masking the store would change what the guest reads back. |
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_minimax.txt:40:- **For `CONF`**: A, and the case is even stronger. The consumer is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` — it looks at bit 0 only. The guard is `idata & ~1` — it fires on any non-bit-0 bit. Under A, a rejected value with reserved bits set is stored, and `bus` correctly reflects bit 0 of what the guest wrote. Under B, the old `ioc_conf` is kept, and `bus` reflects bit 0 of the *previous* write — which may not be what the guest intended. **B can silently corrupt the PCI bus selection.** This is exactly the "silent state corruption" `#447` warns about, but caused *by B itself*, not by A. The brief does not address this asymmetry.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_minimax.txt:66:- **`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa)`, `lca_interrupt_assert()`, `lca_interrupt_deassert()`**: stated to still have `exit(1)` sites. **REASONED** — not inlined; I cannot check.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_minimax.txt:69:- **`LCA_IOC_BASE`** and **`LCA_IOC_TBIA`** are already unguarded (just `break;`). If A is chosen, they need no change; they are already in the A state.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_minimax.txt:78:**Not nameable from this packet.** The LCA chipset (DECchip 21171/21172) is associated with certain Alpha machines; the inlined comment "Linux reads from the base at startup" on `LCA_IOC_BASE` confirms a real guest *read* path exists, but says nothing about which of the six guarded arms a cold boot hits. Whether Linux on an LCA machine writes `0x700000` to `W_MASK0` (or any of the other five) during PCI setup is a question about the kernel's Alpha PCI init, which the packet does not inline.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_minimax.txt:96:**Final disposition**: A for all six, with the understanding that CONF's case for A is *stronger* than the five windows' case, and B is wrong for CONF outright (it can corrupt `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`). The latched-once-print variant of A is worth considering if first-sighting diagnostics matter to the project. The deciding measurement is premise 2 — a grep for fourth reads of the five fields — and that measurement was performed by the author, not by me, and is recorded as REASONED.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_kimi.txt:1:**VERDICT: Premise 6 does not survive contact with Premise 2. The author's own field census gives `ioc_conf` exactly one honored consumer, so "the model consumes these fields for nothing" is false for one arm and A-as-uniform is not licensed. For the other five arms Premise 6 is sound — but the sound version is "#447's ICR cell: no honored state to corrupt," not "the lie is pre-existing." The fork as posed (one answer for the file) repeats the exact error the #447 census inlines a warning against. Disposition: split per arm — decline or mask-and-accept for `LCA_IOC_CONF`; drop-exit-plus-announce for the five pure-storage arms. B is dominated for the five: it makes the register file lie (write X, read back old) while fixing nothing, since the accepted-value lie survives B too.**
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_kimi.txt:6:- P2 (five fields pure storage, `ioc_conf` consumed once): cannot be settled from the packet. The inlined function shows each field's readback+store (2 of 3 occurrences) but not the declaration counts or the fourth `ioc_conf` occurrence. Settling fact: a tree-wide occurrence count per field, or inlining `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;` from `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)` in `dev_lca.c`.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_kimi.txt:15:(a) *Internal contradiction.* P2 says `ioc_conf` is consumed: `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` drives PCI bus selection. CONF's guard condition is `idata & ~1`, so every rejected value has bit 0 equal to the opposite of some accepted value. Concrete case, from the packet's own #506 example: guest writes `0x2` — under A, `ioc_conf = 2` lands, `bus = 0`, PCI routing flips, readback returns `0x2`. The model has now *acted* on a value the implementation explicitly disclaims. That is RCR1's shape: stored state diverges from honored state. Premise 6 ("the model implements window translation for NO value") covers at most the four window registers plus `tlb_enable`. It cannot carry CONF, and A is specified "from all six."
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_kimi.txt:23:REASONED (decidable from the packet *if* P2 is taken as given; the one fact that decides it locally is whether any rejected value reaches an honored consumer — and the one packet-unverifiable fact behind *that* is the `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` consumer in `lca_pci_conf`, named above).
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_kimi.txt:53:REASONED from the packet; addresses not computable from it. The packet contains evidence the device is reached by real traffic — the `LCA_IOC_BASE` arm's comment reads "Linux reads from the base at startup" (READ from inlined code), and the guard values (`0x700000`, `0x300800000`, …) have the shape of observed boot streams rather than spec constants. So at least one in-tree machine plausibly boots an OS through this device. But no machine name, no `LCA_IOC_BASE` numeric value, and no per-register offsets are inlined, so I cannot name the machine or the probe address and will not guess either.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_kimi.txt:55:Two facts settle it, both cheap: (1) which machine file under `GXEMUL-SEC/src/` instantiates `dev_lca` and at what physical base (one grep for the init call); (2) the values of `LCA_IOC_BASE` and `LCA_IOC_W_MASK0` (the header). If both exist, a rung-3 debugger store of any value `!= 0x700000` to `machine_base + (LCA_IOC_W_MASK0 - LCA_IOC_BASE)` reaches the arm through real decode; the probe need not match any real guest's value stream, so envelope violation is free. If no machine instantiates the device, it is documented-only.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_glm.txt:1:**VERDICT: Premise 6 is sound for five of the six arms and FALSE for `LCA_IOC_CONF` — the packet's own premise 2 names a live consumer (`d->ioc_conf & 1` in `lca_pci_conf`), which makes CONF exactly RCR1-shaped and settles CONF → B from the packet alone. For the other five the fork is NOT settleable here: premise 2's counts are asserted, not inlined; one scoped tree grep decides them. If it holds, take accept-plus-announce-once, not bare A — A as specified deletes the model's only runtime record that out-of-set values are unmodelled.**
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_glm.txt:6:- **P2 — stated, NOT CHECKABLE from the packet.** Only `lca_ioc` is inlined; the tree-wide counts and the `lca_pci_conf` consumer line are asserted. The `ioc_conf` count of 4 is internally coherent with the packet (2 inlined uses + declaration + 1 consumer); the fives' counts of 3 are coherent but unproven. Settling fact: a scoped search for reads of `window_base_0/1`, `window_mask_0/1`, `tlb_enable`. One caveat the panel should not skip: a bare grep for `tlb_enable` may hit other devices' fields, so "exactly 3, whole tree" is only meaningful if scoped to `struct lca_data`. Re-run with the scoping stated — this is exactly the class of count this panel ratified wrongly before.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_glm.txt:27:- **`LCA_IOC_CONF` → B.** Deciding fact, in the packet (as stated): `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;`. Honest dependency: this inherits P2; if the consumer were wrong, CONF joins the five. Cost worth weighing: B declines whole writes, so `0x3` discards a legitimate bit-0 request; MASK (store `idata & 1`, announce the rest) avoids that but changes readback policy (P3) and has no precedent here. #447 chose whole-write decline for exactly this shape.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_glm.txt:42:**NOT CHECKABLE from the packet.** No machine list, no `LCA_IOC_BASE` value, no address map, no debugger capability is inlined. Three facts settle it: the numeric value of `LCA_IOC_BASE`; the bus address at which the in-tree Alpha machine registers the IOC (wherever `dev_lca` init is called); whether a rung-3 probe can issue a physical store on Alpha. Outside-packet hardware lore, flagged as such and not to be treated as fact: Linux's `core_lca.h` maps IOC_CONF at physical 0x180000000, so *if* the tree wires the IOC at hardware addresses, a store to 0x180000000 plus the per-register offsets reaches the arms. Whether that "if" holds is precisely the grep above.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_glm.txt:47:- The switch keys on `relative_addr + LCA_IOC_BASE`, so the enum constants double as device-relative offsets and `default:` prints a small relative offset, not an IOC enum value — anyone diffing trip diagnostics should know that.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_deepseek.txt:17:Yes, that is right. The `default:` arm still diagnoses and exits for both reads and writes; `LCA_IOC_STAT0` and `LCA_IOC_STAT1` have no case; and explicit exits remain in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa)`, and the interrupt assert/deassert functions. Nothing else from the packet.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\ollama_deepseek.txt:20:Not checkable from the packet. To settle it, one would need the in-tree Alpha machine that maps the LCA IOC base and the value of `LCA_IOC_BASE`. Without that, no machine or address can be named.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\grok.txt:19:`IOC_CONF` is the existence proof that “no consumer” is already false for 1 of 6: `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`. Under A, a write of `0x2` (rejected extra bit, implemented bit clear) **clears the live bus bit**. Under B it would not. That is the RCR1 shape: a write the guard refuses still mutates implemented state.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\grok.txt:42:**CONF is the one arm where the counter has teeth**, and even there it does not pick B. Extra bits are unused for bus selection (premise 3 holds: `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`, readback is the full stored value). A write of `0x3` under A sets bus 1 — which is what bit 0 asked for — and then `lca_pci_conf` still `exit(1)`s on `bus != 0`. A write of `0x2` under A *does* clear a previously-set bus bit. That is a real A-vs-B delta. It is still closer to a stored register file than to RCR1: the implemented bit is doing what the write said, and the extra bits are unimplemented-feature residue. B would refuse to update bit 0 because an unrelated bit was set.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\grok.txt:67:- **This file’s own two precedents, which split:** `dev >= 21` already deletes a kill and substitutes a default (`dev = 0`) — that is A’s cousin for a *range* site, not a warrant to invent a default window encoding. `LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE) and `TBIA` already silently ignore. T_BASE already accept-and-store. The value guards are the outliers in *this* switch, not the house style.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcabadval\panel_p1_20260907\grok.txt:102:Past the packet: `regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` already issued a guest `stq`/`ldq` through that decode at CONF on unmodified `alphabook1`. The other five are other cases of the same `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)` on the same mapping. Three probe traps that file already paid for, so a new one does not rediscover them: debugger `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; debugger `put d` of a killing value *is* a host kill but is not a guest instruction; HAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`.
