(flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">holdphase</span><span class="ttl">the hold-marker discriminator was implemented with `&gt;=` where the carrier says LATER, and six holds were cleared by SAME-DATE siblings written by the batch that created the marker</span><span class="tip">the hold-marker discriminator was implemented with `&gt;=` where the carrier says LATER, and six holds were cleared by SAME-DATE siblings written by the batch that created the marker — witness: harness:MEASURED by the codex and opus5 seats on the #497b pass-2 panel, independently, and confirmed here row by row before reverting.  SIX markers were wrongly cleared, ALL of them phase=regress / seat=fable5: ns16550, carrier, es438, sh4sci, wdcdataoutdrain, and wdccdbmidxfer entry [26].  *** THIS FIELD PREVIOUSLY NAMED THE SIXTH AS &#x27;research/opus5, answered at review&#x27;.  THAT IS THE MARKER THE COMMIT **KEPT** -- disposition (b) -- NOT the one it cleared.  Caught by the fable5 seat; I had copied the framing from another seat&#x27;s table without checking which entry it pointed at. ***  widthkillclass was the one CORRECT clearance: held at review, all seven named seats answered at review.  A strictly-later + same-stage test keeps all six and clears that one.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gxprobepccache</span><span class="ttl">gxprobe cached the pc across `step`, so a driver that stepped without _exec_at() had its next pc assignment silently skipped -- a detector row that could not fail</span><span class="tip">gxprobe cached the pc across `step`, so a driver that stepped without _exec_at() had its next pc assignment silently skipped -- a detector row that could not fail — witness: harness:MEASURED by the #497-family measuring seat and re-verified here.  `gxprobe.py` skips a redundant register assignment when `cache_regs` is on; `_exec_at()` pops the cached pc after stepping, but a driver calling `send(&quot;step&quot;)` DIRECTLY did not -- so its next `set_reg(&quot;pc&quot;, a)` was dropped as redundant.  The transcript is unambiguous: the first access carries a `pc=` line, the second and third do not, and they execute `nop`.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sgideprimop</span><span class="ttl">dev_sgi_re.c draw_primitive(): a guest-written drawing op ends the host -- 254 of 256 top-byte values reach `default: fatal(); exit(1);`</span><span class="tip">dev_sgi_re.c draw_primitive(): a guest-written drawing op ends the host -- 254 of 256 top-byte values reach `default: fatal(); exit(1);` — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/sgideprimop/witness_20260831.sh -- rung-3 on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`.  DE_PRIM_TRIANGLE (0x02000000) and DE_PRIM_POINT (0x00000000) each kill the host with the op echoed in the diagnostic; DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE (0x03000000) survives with zero kill messages.  The RECTANGLE control is what makes it discriminating rather than merely fatal.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — ADJUDICATION of nine dispositions, verified by opening files rather than by reasoning; it stated its line numbers matched the brief&#x27;s ranges, proving it was in the fork and not a pristine copy.  **Zero dispositions reversed on substance; two corrected.**  *** ITS CENTRAL FINDING PREVENTED THE ROUND FROM MINTING THE DEFECT IT HAD JUST FILED. ***  The natural oracle-correction for `tail_acc_x` is `exit1` -&gt; `exit0` (*the host now survives*).  Traced as a CHANNEL rather than an intent: under a future one-token death reinstatement -- `exit(0)` at the default arm -- the session announces once and dies, `how` is `exit0`, the re-pinned row reads GREEN, `tail_acc` still counts 1, no other session reaches `draw_primitive()`, and the detector scores a **TOTAL ESCAPE, 29/29**.  **Exit status cannot vary between *declined and quit* and *died with status 0*.**  Repair: re-pin onto a positive post-draw observable (a load through the device; `printed()` already reads `died`), keeping an exit-status row only as a second channel.  **L1 -- a row under construction -- so no stopping-rule tension**, and the round owes a FOURTH mutant: death-by-`exit(0)` at the default arm.  *Knowable at writing time from the CHANNEL check, which is exactly why that check runs before the table.*  **Corrected my `exit0oracle` census: 13 sites, not 9** -- my grep anchored on the variable name `how` and missed four rows using `how1`..`how4`.  **Upheld the RANKING with a measurement I did not have**: it built a decline-then-`exit(0)` mutant of `#498` and found `lo_sent` KILLS it via the read-side sentinel even though all four `*_alive` rows pass -- so the blindness is per-ROW, not per-detector, and no shipped detector is floor-breached as a whole.  **Re-verified F5 mechanically rather than accepting my ruling in my own favour**: `DRAW` is a PREFIX of the fatal and `(once)` appends after it, and the `tail_acc` session is a fresh process issuing exactly one draw-triggering access -- so first occurrence = only occurrence = 1, latched or not.  Keep-the-text confirmed, and it noted the current text stays TRUE post-fix (still unimplemented, now declined), so there is no wrong-record pressure to reword.  **One records correction against me on F1:** `#498`&#x27;s own probe header ALREADY carried the reset-value fact in its DOES-NOT-CATCH section.  What is new is the headline promotion and the `memset` verification a packet-fed seat&#x27;s refusal forced -- *the round should credit the header rather than book a fresh discovery*.  Also flagged two records nits, both correct: my &quot;one uncommitted edit&quot; undercounted, and `CHECKPOINT.md`&#x27;s HEAD had gone stale by its own rule. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — THE NINTH SEAT; this stage was held open for it.  **Q1: the precedent is not elsewhere in the tree -- it is INSIDE `draw_primitive()` itself, twice.**  The ROP `default:` arms latch-and-announce without exiting, and the XFER guard in the same function has its `exit(1)` COMMENTED OUT.  So decline + latched announce is the file&#x27;s own idiom and the fix is not an import.  It killed the other three members on facts: ACCEPT needs a computed-then-discarded value and nothing computes a POINT or TRIANGLE; FAULT is an ADDRESS-SPACE disposition and this is a command-register VALUE, with no space to leave unmapped; BACKED ACCEPT fabricates storage where there is no register.  *** Q2 -- A FIFTH MUTANT, AND IT IS A FLOOR OBLIGATION RATHER THAN COVERAGE DEBT. ***  **`M5 primdrop`: delete the `case DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE:` LABEL** so RECTANGLE falls into the de-fatalised `default:`.  One line, and plausible rather than contrived because the four documented primitives INTERLEAVE.  Not covered by M1-M4: guard intact, bit intact, `|=` intact, and NO DEATH at all -- strictly quieter than M4.  **Measured, not argued: `grep` for `PRIMITIVE|0x2060|0x01000000|0x03000000` across all three shipped probes returns ZERO hits -- not one probe in the tree programs the primitive register or issues a legal LINE or RECTANGLE**, so every row I prescribed tests only the DECLINE side and the mutant sweeps all three clean.  **Why it is FLOOR and not debt:** the de-escalation clause requires the detector shipping WITH the fix to pin the ACCEPT SIDE of each predicate the guard gated -- *part of the fix&#x27;s cost*.  **My PRETABLE section 2 enumerated five rows and not one is an accept row.**  Before the fix, deleting that label meant `exit(1)` on the guest&#x27;s first rectangle -- unmissable; after it, one `(once)` line and a console that never renders text, since the file records at `:760-761` that OpenBSD draws characters this way.  **That is the accidental tripwire the fix destroys.**  Kill channel, needing no framebuffer read-back: a legal RECTANGLE with `DE_DRAWMODE_ROP` set and an unimplemented `rop`, asserting `count(DRAW) == 0` AND `count(&quot;[ sgi_de: RECT: rop[&quot;) == 1` -- an ARM-UNIQUE anchor (verified: two hits in the file, LINE and RECT).  Caveat that decides the session shape: `rop_used` is function-static, so the anchor fires once per PROCESS.  *** AND A HAZARD IN THE VERY PRECEDENT THAT LICENSES THE FIX: COPY THE SHAPE, NOT THE SCOPE. ***  `static char rop_used[256]` and `static int first` are FUNCTION-STATIC -- process-global, shared by every device instance, the `n_trunc_msgs` anti-pattern.  Census: **4 lines, ALL in this file, ALL inside `draw_primitive()`** -- the tree&#x27;s only such sites.  `d-&gt;warned` + `(once)` is the house form.  It also recorded a tension rather than resolving it: the in-file precedent latches PER VALUE while this fix latches ONE BIT for 254 op classes, so POINT-then-TRIANGLE prints once.  Deliberate (it is M1&#x27;s killer) but **the round must say why it did not use an `(op &gt;&gt; 24)`-indexed table**, or a reader takes the difference for an oversight.  *Hygiene: it reported that the tree was committed under it twice mid-review, checked that none of the five files it cites changed, and confirmed it wrote nothing.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  **CORRECTED HALF OF MY OWN HEADLINE CLAIM.**  I recorded POINT as what *an uninitialised OR PARTIALLY-WRITTEN op register* produces.  The reset half is proven (`memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct sgi_re_data))` zeroes `de_reg[]`); **the partially-written half is FALSE** -- `#498`&#x27;s width guard declines byte and halfword accesses BEFORE `de_reg[]` is updated, so accepted 32/64-bit writes always store complete words.  *One of this round&#x27;s own fixes closed the door I was citing.*  **DID THE OPTION-SET PRECEDENT GREP the three-membered-set rule demands**, and the tree answers clearly: `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` declines an unimplemented command with a per-instance `(once)` diagnostic and completes normally, `dev_m8820x.c` ignores one; `dev_asc.c` raises a guest interrupt ONLY because it has an explicitly modelled illegal-command channel, which this site does not.  That is why FAULT is wrong here -- an in-tree warrant, not an argument.  *** TWO MORE ONE-CHARACTER MUTANT CLASSES, ONE OF WHICH IS NEW TO THIS PROJECT. ***  (a) `1 &lt;&lt; 7` -&gt; `1 &lt;&lt; 6` aliases an existing bit -- the `#498` collision class, killed by pre-arming all seven existing latch bits in one session before triggering.  (b) **`d-&gt;warned |= SGIRE_W_PRIMOP` -&gt; `d-&gt;warned = SGIRE_W_PRIMOP`, one character, which CLEARS EVERY EARLIER LATCH** -- killed by an A -&gt; primitive -&gt; A session requiring A&#x27;s message exactly once.  It proposes combining them: fire all seven latch sites, trigger POINT, revisit the sites, and require every message exactly once.  *** AND A VACUITY FINDING AGAINST SHIPPED MATERIAL: `exit0` IS NOT A SAFE SURVIVAL ORACLE. ***  *&quot;A wrong `exit(0)` still kills the emulator and satisfies that oracle.&quot;*  `#498`&#x27;s `hi_alive`/`lo_alive`/`lo_alive2` rows all pin `how == &quot;exit0&quot;`.  Assert the `alive` value captured BEFORE the harness sends `quit`, ideally with a post-trigger command.  Filed as its own row -- it is coverage debt against a shipped detector above the floor, not an L2.  **GUEST-CONTROL TRACE, concrete:** RE base `0x15000000`, DE mapping `0x15002000`, `CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE` offset `0x60`, START alias `0x60 | 0x800 = 0x860`, so a guest `sw` to **`0xffffffffb5002860`** with `t0 = 0` draws a POINT.  `startFlag` captures bit `0x800` and is cleared; `regnr = 0x60/4`.  **And `if (startFlag)` is NOT conditioned on `MEM_WRITE`, so a START-aliased READ also invokes drawing** -- worth a row of its own.  Notes that `break`-inside-latch is a non-mutant here: `default` is last and the function has no tail, so moving or deleting that `break` changes nothing. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling.  NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  Checked the tree rather than the packet and said so: noticed HEAD was two commits past the brief&#x27;s `e898c84`, read the queue row, and confirmed **nothing has shipped for this row** -- the same premise error the measuring seat caught on the sibling round.  Independently confirmed the mask/constant table, the no-common-tail claim (*&quot;the switch&#x27;s closing brace is the last statement&quot;*), that `op` is guest-controlled through `CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE` and *is already DE_PRIM_POINT at RESET with no store*, and that bits [31:26] are unused by every `DE_PRIM_` constant so `DE_PRIM_LINE | 0x04000000` dies.  Chose **decline + latched announce**, not FAULT, and ruled the caller should NOT see the decline.  *** ITS BEST FINDING IS A THIRD BLAST-RADIUS TIER THAT BOTH I AND THE PACKET-FED SEATS MISSED. ***  I had corrected the radius to *one certain row (tail_acc_x) and one conditional on wording (tail_acc)*.  grok added: **a REWORD does not RED `hi_draw`/`hi_rd_draw` -- it BLINDS them.**  Both assert `count(DRAW) == 0`, so once the substring no longer exists they stay green *for the wrong reason* -- vacuous by construction, invisible to every gate.  It also caught that my `gate_arm.sh` comment said *both rows* red, *&quot;That comment overstates the same way the brief does&quot;* -- an L4 in shipped material, corrected in place.  **The design constraint that follows is sharper than mine: KEEP THE MESSAGE TEXT and add `(once)`.**  Keeping it costs ONE re-pin; rewording costs two re-pins AND silently blinds two further rows.  Disclosed honestly that it did not re-run the probe -- *&quot;this is a read of the live oracles against the code paths they exercise&quot;* -- which is the READING/MEASUREMENT distinction stated without being asked. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING; went to the tree for everything it could not settle and said which.  *** CAUGHT A REAL DEFECT IN MY BRIEF: I INLINED THE SUPERSEDED LATCH CENSUS. ***  My settled-premises block gave `(once)` 9, `(further occurrences suppressed)` 3, `(reported once)` 2.  **Those are the 2026-08-26 literals that the carrier itself DEMOTES in writing** -- *&quot;Superseded literals, kept only as the demoted record ... Never a current claim&quot;* -- re-derived on 08-30 as 24/4/5.  kimi measured 25/4/5 and I re-ran the carrier&#x27;s own deriver: **25 / 4 / 5**, confirming kimi and refuting my brief.  *The carrier names a deriver precisely so nobody transcribes the number, and I transcribed the one it had crossed out.*  The count grew 24 -&gt; 25 because THIS session&#x27;s `#498` added a `(once)` site -- the rounds that consult the number are the rounds that change it, which is the carrier&#x27;s stated reason for the deriver.  **Its conclusion is unaffected and strengthened: `(once)` is not merely the plurality but the overwhelming majority.**  *** ITS SECOND CORRECTION IS WRONG, AND CHECKING IT VINDICATED THE CARRIER. ***  kimi reports that the tree&#x27;s counter-style outlier *&quot;lives in `src/core/memory.c`&quot;* and that it saw **no counter** there, only a verbosity gate with a probe-tolerance early-out.  Measured: `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` DOES carry `static int n_trunc_msgs = 0;` with `n_trunc_msgs &lt; 8` and `n_trunc_msgs ++` -- a real counter, exactly as the carrier describes.  **kimi found a DIFFERENT diagnostic** -- `memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr()` in `core/memory.c`, which `memory_rw.c` calls and which genuinely has no counter.  Two similar-looking diagnostics in one area; it searched for the described behaviour and landed on the wrong one.  *Recorded rather than quietly dropped: a seat&#x27;s correction carries authority, and this one would have sent a future round to rewrite an accurate carrier paragraph.*  *** AND A THIRD Q4 OBSERVER THAT grok AND codex BOTH MISSED: THE HARDCODED ROW DENOMINATOR. ***  `regress/gate_arm.sh` checks `sgi_de rows green` against a literal `29`.  When `tail_acc_x` reds, that count falls to 28 and **the denominator check reds too** -- so the oracle-correction re-pin must bump the literal in the same edit, or the round meets two reds and attributes one of them wrongly.  Neither other file-reading seat named it. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  *** THE ROUND&#x27;S BEST FINDING AND IT LANDS AGAINST THE AUTHOR: the 2-of-4 reframing is a PARTITION of the 254/256 claim, not a correction, and recording only the sharper half is the locally-true restatement that hides the defect. ***  Also asked the one checkable caveat nobody else did -- are op bits [31:26] live? -- which on checking WIDENED the defect: the primitive field is bits [25:24] and the mask is [31:24], so a legal LINE or RECTANGLE with a stray high bit also dies.  Named four premises as not settleable from the packet, each with the single fact that would settle it, and separated the all-zeroes CONSTANT from the register&#x27;s RESET VALUE. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Supplied the clause-(d) mutant BEFORE the fix exists -- drop the `!` from the guard, so the latch never sets and the announce never fires while the bit name and the absent exit(1) both still read correct -- AND the row that kills it: two distinct unimplemented primitives in one session, expecting exactly one message.  Precision correction: 254/256 counts MASKED TOP-BYTE CLASSES, not full 32-bit op values. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Refused the no-common-tail premise and answered CONDITIONALLY ON BOTH BRANCHES, naming #498&#x27;s `goto`-to-reach-the-tail as the governing precedent if a tail exists.  Marked the uninitialised-register inference as REASONED rather than READ -- the distinction the author had blurred. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pcc2widthkills</span><span class="ttl">dev_pcc2.c: TEN live width kills (of twelve guards -- #119 already fixed two in this same file, and its shape is the answer)</span><span class="tip">dev_pcc2.c: TEN live width kills (of twelve guards -- #119 already fixed two in this same file, and its shape is the answer) — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/pcc2widthkills/witness_20260831.sh -- a RUNG-3 witness. Real m88k guest instructions through real decode on unmodified `-E mvme88k -e mvme187`: the trace shows `st r1,r2,0x0 ; [0xfff4201c] = 0x00000042` followed by `TODO: pcc2: non-byte reads and writes of PCCTWO_SCCERR`.  Built by the kimi seat, REPRODUCED independently by the coordinator with a `put b` negative control and a `put w` positive control.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Reached the 5-of-10 split independently of kimi, from the alignment rule plus the fact that `dev_pcc2_access` switches on the transaction&#x27;s STARTING offset.  **Refuted my settled premise 5**: a `memcpy` runs BEFORE the switch on the read path, so these are not value-declines at all -- in-range wide reads return the register span and end-crossing reads are zero-filled. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING, and it settled the question that decides whether this row is a defect or documentation -- **against me, and in the direction that makes the row WORSE**.  *** Q1: WIDE ACCESS IS REACHABLE.  My premise was that these are byte-wide control registers and the CPU or bus glue might be unable to deliver a 2- or 4-byte access at all.  Refuted twice over, both verified here. ***  **(a) The tree&#x27;s OWN header documents the legality of what is being killed.**  `src/include/thirdparty/mvme_pcctworeg.h:9-10`: *&quot;PCCchip2 control and status register can be accessed as bytes (8 bits), two-bytes (16 bits), or four-bytes (32 bits).&quot;*  So **byte-wide is a property of this MODEL&#x27;s `switch (relative_addr)`, not of the chip the header describes** -- the guards kill on accesses the hardware documentation in this very tree says are legal.  **(b) The CPU delivers them.**  `src/cpus/cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c` is compiled for `LS_SIZE` 1, 2, 4 and 8, declares `uint8_t data[LS_SIZE]` and passes that size to `cpu-&gt;memory_rw`, faulting on misalignment rather than narrowing the access.  So `len` in {1,2,4,8} reaches the device.  *This is the third time tonight a seat has refuted a premise of mine, and the second where the refutation RAISED severity rather than lowering it.*  **Q2: DO NOT convert `#119`&#x27;s two width latches in this round** -- that belongs to `staticlatchscope`.  A clean scoping answer to a question I had genuinely not decided.  **Q3: A RUNG-3 PROBE IS POSSIBLE, with an address**: the debugger&#x27;s `put h` / `put w` reaches `dev_pcc2_access` at `0xfff42000 + off`.  That discharges the attempt this row owes with a concrete invocation rather than a maybe.  **Q4: the smallest survivors of a *host-lived + message-appeared* detector are a SHARED LATCH BIT across the twelve arms, and WARN-THEN-FALL-THROUGH** -- i.e. the detector must pin per-arm identity and the refusal&#x27;s EFFECT, not merely that something was announced. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** STOPPED READING AND MEASURED.  Killed the host twice on the unmodified tree -- once by direct `put w`, once through REAL GUEST DECODE (hand-assembled m88k words, pc=0x1000, step 4), with the trace showing `st r1,r2,0x0 ; [0xfff4201c] = 0x00000042` then the SCCERR kill.  Reproduced independently by the coordinator. ***  Also found the probe-authorship trap that outlives this row -- **`put b` never reaches the device** (0 messages vs 1 for `put w`), because the debugger&#x27;s byte path passes NO_EXCEPTIONS and `memory_rw` skips the device call for DM_DEFAULT devices -- and measured that a wide read from an UNGUARDED base spanning guarded registers is ALREADY ACCEPTED today. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** THE PANEL&#x27;S BEST ANSWER AND ITS THREE CONCLUSIONS SPLIT THREE WAYS, which is the argument for auditing premises rather than only derivations. ***  (a) Refused my unshown claim that `fatal()` returns and named its body as the settling fact -- CHECKED, it returns, so my premise was right and unsupported.  (b) Challenged `grep -c &#x27;^ *exit(1);&#x27;` as blind to other spellings -- the count held at 13, **but the four extra `exit(` hits are `#119`&#x27;s own comments, which is how the fourfold in-file precedent was found at all**.  (c) REFUTED my Q3 premise outright: I claimed the read path assigns `odata`; it appears twice in the file and both are in a DIFFERENT access function, so a declined read returns the caller&#x27;s pre-set value and the ACCEPT option&#x27;s premise is false here. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Ended on the point that matters for the detector: a shared message format across twelve arms makes it easy to keep the message while changing the behaviour -- i.e. rows that check only *a message appeared* are the ones a mutant will walk through. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Landed on the same conclusion I reached from the source: the in-file precedent&#x27;s shape is right and its latching is not.  Flagged the unlatched announce as a defect of the precedent rather than a feature to copy. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lcabadval</span><span class="ttl">dev_lca.c: FOUR chipset registers kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set -- the #443 BADVAL class</span><span class="tip">dev_lca.c: FOUR chipset registers kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set -- the #443 BADVAL class — witness: harness:ENUMERATED from the shipped source, 2026-08-31.  Eleven live terminating calls.  The value guards, each ending in exit(1): `idata &amp; ~1` (:206), `idata &amp; ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` (:226), `idata != 0ULL &amp;&amp; idata != 0x300800000ULL` (:241), `idata != 0x700000ULL` (:256), `idata != 0x240000000ULL` (:280), `idata != 0x3ff00000ULL` (:295).  Each diagnostic says so in its own words -- *&quot;(0x%&quot; PRIx64&quot;) from the only implemented value&quot;*.  Plus two range guards that also kill: `dev &gt;= 21` (:129) and `ofs &gt; len` (:163).  NO rung-3 witness: READ, not booted.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: codex, grok, kimi, opus5, fable5]  Three packet-fed seats answered and settled what a packet can settle -- the accept-set census, and (once I checked it) which fields are consumed.  The rest needs the tree. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** THE ROUND&#x27;S BEST ANSWER.  Refuted the row&#x27;s own title without needing the one fact it could not settle, and caught my brief contradicting itself between Q2 and Q3 -- do these fields feed decode or does the emulator not model them?  Both were asserted. ***  The source settles it against me: base/mask/tlb_enable are written and read back and nothing else; only `ioc_conf` is consumed (`bus = d-&gt;ioc_conf &amp; 1`).  **That splits the fix design** -- three arms are pure storage where a decline may keep the store, and the fourth is not.  Also the sharpest design point of the panel: for `W_BASE0` the accepted `0` is the MAXIMALLY ORDINARY write, so an accept row pinned to the exotic magic constant misfires on the most legal write rather than an unusual one.  Named `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` as the single fact that would close the census, which is exactly the right move for a packet-fed seat. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Derived the accept sets independently and reached the same correction.  Held the line on reachability: named an LCA IOC register specification or PROM source as the one thing that would settle whether these are hardware-legal values or merely the ones this emulator was developed against.  There is no such document in `_scratchpad/`. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Went arm by arm and attached a single named settling fact to each rather than answering around the gaps -- the discipline this project asks of a packet-fed seat. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row&#x27;s own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm&#x27;s text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth&#x27;s printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger&#x27;s earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four. — evidence: [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — RUNG-3 ATTEMPT: MADE 2026-08-31 per the owner ruling, by a reading seat.  *** RESULT: A WITNESS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE AND A FULL SEQUENCE IS SPECIFIED -- so this row is NOT documented-only, and `none&#x27; would have meant `did not think to ask&#x27;. ***  Target `-E alpha -e alphabook1 0x4000:/dev/null&#x27;; machine_alpha.c&#x27;s ST_ALPHABOOK1 is the ONLY subtype calling device_add(machine,&quot;lca&quot;).  Construction and debugger-prompt arrival are ALREADY MEASURED elsewhere in this repo -- CHANGELOG #279&#x27;s blast-radius gate lists alphabook1 among the twelve machines reaching the prompt, and gate_asan_sweep.sh&#x27;s AB_EXEMPT lists alpha/3000/300 and NOT alphabook1.  Guest VA is K0SEG (paddr | 0xfffffc0000000000, memory_alpha.c), with the ptbr==0 identity path as a second route.  A guest `stq a1,0(a0)&#x27; = 0xB6300000 reaches DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) via LS_GENERIC_N -&gt; memory_rw.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress\artifacts\lcaconfwidth\codex_census_20260907.err:1238:GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28118:   "state_note": "*** RECORDS CORRECTION 2026-09-06, from RE-DERIVING this row's own citations instead of trusting them.  THE\nHEADLINE COUNT (eleven live terminating calls) IS RIGHT.  THE ENUMERATION UNDER IT IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, AND THE\nFIRST ONE CHANGES THE FIX DESIGN. ***\n\n**1.  `dev >= 21` IS NOT A LIVE KILL.  IT IS ALREADY DE-FATALISED -- AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN FIX PRECEDENT.**\n\nThe row lists it among \"two range guards that also kill\".  The source says otherwise: the guard is at `:127`, its\nbody is a COMMENTED-OUT `fatal()` + `exit(1)` at `:128-129`, and what actually executes is `dev = 0;` at `:130`.\n\n```c\n\tif (dev >= 21) {\n\t\t/*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);  */\n\t\tdev = 0;\n\t}\n```\n\nSomeone has already made, in this very file, the decision this row exists to make: **delete the kill, substitute a\nsafe default, keep going.**  That is a TRANSFERRED WARRANT from inside the same file, which the six-member\noption-set rule says to go looking for -- *\"grep the fork for an existing idiom; the tree's own precedents are part\nof the design space\"* -- and which nobody had found because the row was read rather than re-derived.  So only ONE\nrange guard is live: `ofs > len` at `:163`.\n\n**2.  THE ENUMERATION COVERED 8 OF THE 11, AND THE THREE IT MISSED ARE NOT VALUE GUARDS AT ALL.**\n\nRe-derived by `grep -n 'exit(1)' dev_lca.c` minus the commented one (12 matches, 11 live), the eleven are:\n\n| line | class | condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 73  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_assert` -- bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)`, **NO GUARD** |\n| 85  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_deassert` -- likewise |\n| 134 | BUS | `bus != 0`, i.e. `d->ioc_conf & 1` |\n| 163 | RANGE | `ofs > len` |\n| 206 | VALUE | `idata & ~1` (IOC_CONF) |\n| 226 | VALUE | `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` |\n| 241 | VALUE | `idata != 0 && idata != 0x300800000` |\n| 256 | VALUE | `idata != 0x700000` |\n| 280 | VALUE | `idata != 0x240000000` |\n| 295 | VALUE | `idata != 0x3ff00000` |\n| 315 | CATCH-ALL | `default:` -- any unimplemented ioc offset, read OR write |\n\n**THE TWO INTERRUPT STUBS ARE PLAUSIBLY THE MOST SEVERE OF THE ELEVEN, AND A ROW FRAMED AS A VALUE-GUARD CLASS\nCOULD NOT SEE THEM.**  They are not a wrong value being written -- they are an ENTIRE UNIMPLEMENTED PATH with no\npredicate at all, so *any* device asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it\nwrites.  The four \"accepts exactly one value\" registers need a firmware revision that writes something else; these\ntwo need only that an interrupt is asserted.\n\n*A row named after one class hides the members of the others.  That is this project's cell-not-class pattern\nwearing an enumeration instead of a detector -- the same shape the m88kexcdeliver chain hit four times.*\n\n**WHAT IS UNCHANGED:** the severity (HIGH), the single consumer (`machine_alpha.c`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`),\nthe absence of any alpha rig, and the observation that a guest here is FIRMWARE so the reaching value is whatever\nSRM or NetBSD writes.  The scope caveat about range-vs-value guards also stands and is now sharper, because there\nis only one live range guard to reason about.\n\n--- the original note follows, unaltered ---\n\n\n=== DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-06 (codex / GPT-6 Astra at max, read-only).  Answer preserved at\n=== regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt.  IT CORRECTED THREE OF THE FIVE PREMISES I HAD\n=== JUST RE-DERIVED, and one of the corrections changes what this round may even choose from.\n\n**SEVERITY ORDER CONFIRMED: the two INTERRUPT STUBS first** -- but \"provisionally; firmware execution order is\nunproved\", which is the honest qualifier my own framing lacked.\n\n*** A SEVENTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: IMPLEMENT. ***  The carrier's set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+LATCHED\nANNOUNCE / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT -- all six presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION.  For the two\ninterrupt stubs none of them applies: *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete.\"*  A stub is not a\nrefusal to service a value; it is an unimplemented PATH, and the disposition is to write the code.  This is the\noption-set completeness rule paying off exactly as it is meant to -- the member was found by a seat reading the\ntree, not by argument.\n\n*** AND THE FAULT MEMBER IS NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE AT ALL. ***  `dev_unreadable.c` works because\n`memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access **for MIPS and M88K, with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT**.\nSo the idiom this fork installs ten times in `machine_sgi.c` does not transfer here, and any round reaching for\nFAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  *A precedent that is real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby\ntransferable; that is the warrant-transfer rule, and it took a seat opening `memory_rw.c` to see it.*\n\n**AND A FINDING ABOUT THE ALPHA PORT THAT OUTRANKS THIS ROW:** the Alpha interrupt callbacks merely set\n`irq_asserted`, and **the execution loop never consumes that flag**.  So implementing the two stubs would not by\nitself deliver an interrupt -- removing the exits cannot complete the path.  Filed as its own question rather\nthan folded in here.\n\n## THE PREMISE AUDIT, and I was wrong three times\n\n* **PREMISE 1 HOLDS.**  Twelve textual occurrences, eleven outside comments.\n* **PREMISE 2's REMOVAL IS REAL; ITS \"USABLE PRECEDENT\" CONCLUSION IS WRONG.**  I read the commented-out\n  `dev >= 21` kill plus its live `dev = 0` as the file's own answer, transferable to this round.  It is not: it\n  **ALIASES \"no selected device\" to DEVICE ZERO**, which is harmless today only because device 7 is the sole\n  installed device and slot 0 happens to be empty.  Populate slot 0 and it is a latent alias defect.  *So the\n  question I asked -- is this a sound precedent or a latent defect of its own -- has the second answer, and my\n  brief had already leaned on the first.*\n* **PREMISE 3's CLASS COUNTS HOLD, ITS SINGLETON CLAIM DOES NOT.**  `W_BASE0` accepts **TWO** values, `0` and\n  `0x300800000` (`idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`), so \"four registers accepting exactly ONE value\" is\n  wrong -- only **three** window guards accept a single value.  *This one is mine twice over: the original row\n  note said it, I re-derived the enumeration tonight and reproduced it, and the guard's own text was in my\n  terminal when I did.*\n* **PREMISE 4 HOLDS** as one machine-setup consumer (AlphaBook 1), with no dedicated boot rig -- **but\n  \"never exercised\" DOES NOT FOLLOW**: a generic constructor sweep exists (`gate_asan_sweep.sh`), without\n  proving coverage of these branches.  My \"no rig means no coverage\" was too strong.\n* **PREMISE 5 IS AN UNSUPPORTED RESTRICTION.**  I said a guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is\n  whatever SRM or NetBSD writes.  Guest KERNEL stores use the same MMIO path, and interrupt callbacks arise from\n  DEVICE events -- neither is firmware.\n\n## DISPOSITIONS IT WOULD TAKE, per class\n* **INTERRUPT STUB (2):** IMPLEMENT forwarding and CPU interrupt delivery.\n* **BUS (1):** implement Type-1 decoding then ACCEPT through `bus_pci`, using its existing absent-device\n  handling.  `ioc_conf & 1` selects the CONFIGURATION-CYCLE TYPE and is **not the decoded bus number** -- and\n  deleting this exit alone leaves `dev` uninitialised.\n* **RANGE (1):** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with initialised read results and no forwarded side effects.  Note\n  `ofs` is at most three, **so this guard cannot fire for four- or eight-byte accesses** -- changing `>`\n  mechanically is not a design.\n* **VALUE (6):** ACCEPT documented register fields using the existing backing, latched announce for the missing\n  DMA/TLB effects.  **Every guarded assignment already happens BEFORE its check**, so no invented backing is\n  needed -- but readback alone does not implement translation.\n* **CATCH-ALL (1):** split documented registers (`STAT0`/`STAT1`) from genuine holes; FAULT only for confirmed\n  holes and only once Alpha exception delivery exists.\n\n**WHAT IT COULD NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly:** source shows connected interrupt paths and conditional MMIO paths\nfor the other nine sites, but **no particular SRM/NetBSD execution of any terminating site**.  Firmware\nreachability and failure frequency remain unmeasured, so the severity order is provisional on exactly that.\n\n\n*** THE SEVERITY WARRANT THIS ROW WAS MISSING: THE REJECTED VALUES INCLUDE A DOCUMENTED ONE, AND THE\nSTORE LANDS BEFORE THE PROCESS ENDS.  Found by the codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt),\nverified against the source and the header before recording. ***\n\nEach of the three window guards has this shape:\n\n    d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS\n    if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends\n\n**And zero is a documented encoding.**  From src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:\n\n    #define IOC_W_MASK_1M   0x0000000000000000ULL   /* 1MB window */\n\nSo a guest selecting the SMALLEST DOCUMENTED WINDOW SIZE ends the emulator.  The same argument covers\nW_BASE1, whose one accepted constant 0x240000000 carries IOC_W_BASE_WEN (0x200000000, *Window enable*) --\n**so a driver DISABLING a window, by writing a base with WEN clear, is killed too.**\n\n*** THIS IS #504's WARRANT AT A SECOND DEVICE: documented values that the model kills on. ***  It is not a\njunk-value guard; it is a whitelist of ONE that excludes documented members of its own encoding.  codex adds\nthat all three registers start at zero through constructor initialisation, so the rejected value is also the\nRESET value.\n\n**AND THE RANGE SITE IS FAR MORE REACHABLE THAN THIS ROW SAID.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len'.  Reading\nDEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;' -- **ofs is guest ADDRESS BITS 5-6**,\nnot a written value.  So an ordinary 1-byte access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the\nprocess.  That is address decoding, not a value whitelist, and it belongs in a different disposition class\nfrom the six value guards.  The rest of that function is filed separately as [[lcaisalane]].\n\n*** DISPOSITION PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition_20260907.txt).  IT NARROWS MY REACHABILITY CLAIM,\nCONFIRMS TWO OTHERS, AND SUPPLIES THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR -- INCLUDING\nCONFLICTING ONES, WHICH IS WHAT I ASKED IT TO SURFACE. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Disposition 5 is unavailable through Alpha's failed-device-access path.  Five candidate rounds fit\na uniform VALUE-refusal policy.  The tree supplies CONFLICTING precedents, especially for lane handling and\nstoring unsupported values.\"*\n\n*** MY `AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE ENDS THE EMULATOR' CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND I HAVE REPEATED IT IN THREE\nPLACES. ***  codex: *\"they read zero INITIALLY, then echo accepted writes.  Writing back the initial zero\nkills the last three; an arbitrary RMW does not necessarily kill them -- its resulting value matters.\"*\n\n**The precise claim is the INITIAL-ZERO readback**: before any accepted write, `W_MASK0', `W_BASE1' and\n`W_MASK1' read 0 and accept only one non-zero constant each, so a read-modify-write performed on a\nfreshly-reset device writes 0 back and ends the process.  **Once a valid value has been written the register\nechoes it**, and an RMW of that value is accepted.  So the window is early -- driver probe or\ninitialisation -- rather than any RMW at any time.  That is still a routine thing for a driver to do at\nattach, and it is still reached with a documented encoding (`IOC_W_MASK_1M` = 0), but the sentence I wrote\nwas wider than the fact.\n\n**CONFIRMED, both of which I had asserted:** every VALUE arm STORES BEFORE CHECKING; and the accepted sets\nare exactly as filed (`IOC_CONF' {0,1}, `IOC_TB_ENA' {0,0x80}, `W_BASE0' {0,0x300800000}, and the three\nsingle-value ones).\n\n**DISPOSITION 5 IS UNAVAILABLE -- now established from a third direction.**  The carrier already recorded it\ngenerally, [[alphafaultclaim]] reached it from the instruction side, and this reaches it from the\nfailed-device-access path.  **Any proposal for a bus-error refusal on an Alpha device owes Alpha exception\ndelivery first**, which is a far larger round than the one it would be riding on.\n\n**THE PRECEDENT I ASKED FOR, AND IT IS AN EXACT ONE:** `cpu_sh.c' defines `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()' and\n`sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()' with EMPTY `/* TODO */' bodies -- *\"an exact unimplemented-callback precedent\nfor 2, ignore silently\"* -- and `core/interrupt.c' defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert' callbacks,\nthough those represent intentionally absent connections rather than unimplemented ones.  **So the two\nINTERRUPT STUBS have a direct in-tree answer** and do not need disposition 7 after all, which is what I had\nassumed they needed.\n\n**AND IT REPORTS THE PRECEDENTS CONFLICT** -- especially on lane handling and on whether to store an\nunsupported value.  That is the more useful answer: it means the choice is genuinely open at those sites\nrather than settled by the tree, so a round there must argue rather than transfer.\n\n**SCOPE: FIVE candidate rounds** under a uniform VALUE-refusal policy -- consistent with the earlier read's\n*\"this needs several rounds\"* and finer than it.\n\n**WHERE THE OVER-BROAD FORM SURVIVES, named because it cannot be edited:** commit `de96f11`'s MESSAGE\nsays *\"each reads back 0 while its guard accepts one non-zero constant, and AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE\nENDS THE EMULATOR on all three\"*.  A commit message is immutable, so the house rule -- correct the false\nrecord IN PLACE -- cannot be followed literally.  **The next best thing is to point at it from the record a\nreader will reach**, which is this row.  The narrowed claim above is the operative one: the kill needs the\nINITIAL zero readback, not any RMW at any time."
